# Lock line endings on test fixtures so cross-platform checkout doesn't
# silently mutate test inputs. Tests assert exact `content` and `line`
# fields, so any CRLF↔LF translation by git's autocrlf would break them.
tests/fixtures/*.md text eol=lf

# Exception: the CRLF fixture must preserve its CRLF line endings on every
# platform. Mark as binary so git never normalises it.
tests/fixtures/crlf.md binary

# Brand asset formats tracked by src/manifest.ts (see SUPPORTED_FORMATS).
# Raster formats get a global `binary` rule (== `-text -diff`): there is no
# useful text diff for PNG/JPG/WEBP anywhere in this repo, so disabling git's
# text-vs-binary content-sniffing heuristic everywhere is strictly safer with
# no downside, and simpler than scoping it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary

# *.svg is scoped to logos/** ONLY -- do NOT widen this to a bare `*.svg`.
# SVG is genuine text (XML), so marking it binary looks like a mistake at
# first glance, but it is deliberate exactly where it applies. manifest.ts's
# generateManifest() only ever scans logosDir (default "logos" -- see the
# --logos option default in src/cli.ts), and every asset it finds under
# there gets a SHA-256 in manifest.json that `brand verify` checks byte for
# byte. This rig runs with core.autocrlf=true, a common Windows default;
# without this rule, checkout would rewrite a tracked logos/**/*.svg's line
# endings, changing its hash and failing `brand verify` on a clean,
# untampered clone. It is latent today (0 svg/webp assets exist under
# logos/ yet) and becomes live the day anyone adds one.
#
# Site SVGs (site/public/favicon.svg, site/src/assets/logo.svg, and any
# others under site/**) are NEVER read by manifest.ts -- assets/** isn't
# either, every CLI command defaults --logos to the literal "logos" dir --
# so a bare `*.svg binary` would cost reviewers readable diffs on those
# files for zero correctness benefit. Keeping the rule scoped to logos/**
# preserves text diffs everywhere the hash-stability argument doesn't apply.
logos/**/*.svg binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Git LFS -- model-channel assets only. Ratified by the Director 2026-08-04;
# reasoning in docs/model-channels-spec.md.
#
# SCOPE IS DELIBERATE AND NARROW: `logos/<slug>/model/**` binaries only. The
# existing logos (readme.<ext> and gallery/**) stay as ordinary git blobs and
# must NOT be migrated -- converting them would rewrite history, which is the
# exact cost LFS was chosen to avoid. This repo's packed history is already
# ~281 MiB; these rules are forward-looking and apply to files that do not
# exist yet.
#
# Why LFS and not plain commits or Release assets: a plain-committed model
# binary has no compensator short of `git filter-repo` + force push (which
# invalidates every clone), and Release assets would break three shipped
# commands -- `verify` hashes working-tree bytes (manifest.ts readFileSync),
# `remove` swaps a real directory, `history` walks git paths, and a release
# asset has none of those. LFS keeps all three working: paths stay in git for
# `history`, and the smudge filter gives `verify`/`remove` real bytes.
#
# `-text` (part of the standard LFS filter set) carries the SAME hash-
# stability argument as the logos/**/*.svg rule above: this rig runs with
# core.autocrlf=true, and any line-ending rewrite on a tracked asset changes
# its SHA-256 and fails `brand verify` on a clean, untampered clone.
#
# Failure mode, deliberately loud: a clone made without git-lfs gets pointer
# files, so `brand verify` hashes a ~130-byte pointer and reports a mismatch.
# That is the safe direction -- a noisy failure rather than a silent one.
#
# view.json is NOT tracked here, by extension rather than by exclusion: it is
# small text read by both the CLI and the site build, and it must diff.
#
# Quota (measured 2026-08-04): mcp-tool-shop-org is on the `free` plan ->
# 10 GiB LFS storage + 10 GiB/month bandwidth, metered billing past that.
# Bandwidth is consumed by CI checkouts, not by site visitors. QUOTA-CHECK is
# pre-registered in the spec before any multi-subject rollout.
logos/*/model/*.glb  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
logos/*/model/*.png  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
logos/*/model/*.webp filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
